PEAT now reuses data it already collected from a device during a run instead of requesting it again: HTTP pages (shared by all modules), TLS certificates and FTP file downloads. Telnet banners and FTP welcome messages are also kept, so modules can use them without connecting again. The in-memory cache is thread-safe, size-limited, keeps data for 5 minutes by default, is never written to disk, and is not used during pushes. It's configured with the ``cache_enabled``, ``cache_ttl``, ``cache_max_entries`` and ``cache_max_bytes`` options, can be turned off for a run with ``--no-cache`` (or ``--cache-ttl 0``), and its statistics are logged at the end of a run and saved as ``cache_stats`` in the state dump.
